Z||000000||Haze_Performance||05-03-2001||02:29 AM||||What do you think?<P>I have 4x10 Kenwoods in the dash,<P>Kenwood 4x6's in the rear panels<P>& Rodney's new sub in the stock location. <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com/n60.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rodneydickman.com/n60.html</A>  <P>Im looking for a amp to power it all.<P>Take a look: <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com/cgi-bin/S-IqnqtpdtNMQ/ProdView.asp?s=0&c=3&g=120&I=158XM405EQ&o=M&a=0" TARGET=_blank>http://www.crutchfield.com/cgi-bin/S-IqnqtpdtNMQ/ProdView.asp?s=0&c=3&g=120&I=158XM405EQ&o=M&a=0</A>  <P>What do you think?? I know that the sub wont take all the power... Ill have to keep it tuned down alittle...  <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">    </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><P><A HREF="http://www.clubs.yahoo.com/clubs/ws6fiero" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero Club</A>||||reg||10
Z||000001||stimpy||05-03-2001||09:26 AM||||100 watts will turn that 5" "subwoofer" to confetti! All you are left with is 40w x 4, and $400 seems awful high for that.<BR>Now I know it's not audiophile grade equipment, but I have a Phase Linear 200w amp driving my fronts. $75 at Cicuit City. You could get 2 and still have money left over to get the install done. Just a thought. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ThatRickGuy||05-03-2001||09:47 AM||||A good 5 in sub can handle 100 watts, but it has to be in the correct freq. I would suggest going w/ 2 seperate amps. a 4 channel for your dash and columns, and a single or dual(bridged) for the sub, for the amp sub you'll want something w/ a high pass cut off, that way, you can keep the sub running happily in it's freq range.<P>for the sub, you could run a 100watt amp, the 100 is peek, but if you turn it down a bit, and cut out the highs, it should be fine . One thing that bothers me a bit is that rodney's sub is an 8ohm speaker, I personally would try to find a quality 2 or 4 ohm, you may pay a little more, but it'll sound better and hit harder.<P>For the others, it matters on the specifics. Kenwood has the KFC-415C 4x10s 4ohm 160watt speakers for $100-$150. Good speakers hard hitting, pakage them with KFC-X468C 4x6 4ohm 100 watt 2-way speakers, a good 250-300 watt 4-way amp should be able to handle it all. and you'll get some pretty sweet sound. Importantly, do not go mixing 8ohm and 4 ohm speakers on the same amp... it'll sound bad and you risk your gear in doing so. <P>If fiero sound is still arround, he can give you awesome advise, much better then the rough estimates I can come up with.<p>[This message has been edited by ThatRickGuy (edited 05-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||05-03-2001||10:19 AM||||First, Buy the amp from here and save over $120.00 !!!! (Price is $279.00 as opposed to the $399 at Crutchfield)) <A HREF="http://www.onlinecarstereo.com/caraudio/category.asp?c=16&m=1" TARGET=_blank>http://www.onlinecarstereo.com/caraudio/category.asp?c=16&m=1</A> <P>As for using it with the small sub, You will have to crank the gain way down since the 5" sub is in a ported box. The speaker is 60W RMS and you'll be probably putting in about 50W max. You could also try and make a small sealed box and try it out later to see how it works but you'll lose some low end in the sound. The good news is that if you decide later to upgrade the sub to a larger woofer, you won't need to buy another amp. The 40W's to the other speakers is fine. When setting the amp up I would set the sub level first, then the others so you can get the sound levels equal.<P>I fired up the system today!!! It sounds Great!!!!<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <P>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-03-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-03-2001).]||||reg||1
